17.0$ miles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b9c0ab8-60db-5c9c-a481-2fd656b8e224" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Margin of Error of a Population Proportion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050899 :topic/math-academy-id 6587 :topic/title "Interpreting Margin of Error"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baafdbc4-c0ee-5225-b528-a929e7d90e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227173} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb1a00f-1865-5d91-992f-998ae6954fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227174} {:answer/id #uuid "f9eaba36-6ae8-53a3-8c5e-feefb9934745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227175} {:answer/id #uuid "2f200270-393f-5aae-b33d-bbf82680b59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227176} {:answer/id #uuid "55f33461-50e2-50bc-95fd-839b1aa16234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227177}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2a961fb2-dc4f-5d54-8089-e3f9caf2743b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227172}] :question/id #uuid "29ff5507-aab0-59c2-91da-da56e93450fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-306844" :question/problem "A factory wants to estimate the percentage of smartphone batteries produced in a day that are defective. A random sample of $420$ batteries from that day was inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that $18%$ of all batteries produced that day are defective, with a margin of error of $2.7%$.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions about all batteries produced that day is most reasonable?\n\n1. It is plausible that between $15.3%$ and $20.7%$ of the batteries are defective.\n2. Exactly $18%$ of the batteries are defective.\n3. It is plausible that more than $20.7%$ of the batteries are defective.\n4. Exactly $2.7%$ of the batteries are defective."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3fed1f-bbb1-5e59-bc10-2b672a4994dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Margin of Error of the Mean" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050899 :topic/math-academy-id 6587 :topic/title "Interpreting Margin of Error"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc76260-e8ed-5af9-88a4-451684ef167d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227179} {:answer/id #uuid "bdae6664-6e2f-5d11-98fc-ee5f74ea12b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227180} {:answer/id #uuid "f0924efb-9052-57bf-bf87-52f5d73cda7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227181} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5231d-1e80-5741-b508-5a65b4101cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227182} {:answer/id #uuid "4343e272-2bff-552a-a64e-e0fe024f2584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227183}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab49dd36-5020-5157-bdb9-fd073abc44e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227178}] :question/id #uuid "124b6513-3be4-524e-b5a9-b52277ca8089" :question/math-academy-id "q-306845" :question/problem "In a study, a biologist estimated that the mean weight of a certain species of fish in a lake is $7.8$ kilograms, with a margin of error of $0.4$ kilograms.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\n\n1. It is not possible that the true mean weight of the fish is less than $7.4$ kilograms or greater than $8.2$ kilograms.\n2. It is plausible that the true mean weight of the fish is between $7.4$ kilograms and $8.2$ kilograms.\n3. Every fish in the lake must weigh between $7.4$ kilograms and $8.2$ kilograms.\n4. The true mean weight of the fish is exactly $7.8$ kilograms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de1c6f9-6894-52e6-9a03-a2ebbb6db6ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Speed-Time Graphs" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049307 :topic/math-academy-id 6613 :topic/title "Interpreting Time-Series Graphs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2326a24-1cf3-5ac9-8759-2cdc0dfc932a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227203} {:answer/id #uuid "e632873b-8857-5852-a148-a99e79cf5cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227204} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba4f305-a4eb-5929-8bbf-ff146b97b0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227205} {:answer/id #uuid "295e6bb2-e110-571e-938e-9b32547571c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227206} {:answer/id #uuid "64c34112-3353-5d2a-92f5-bf6adc8bece0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227207}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b51ef000-e837-53e4-bdde-77999289e925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227202}] :question/id #uuid "df0858ff-53b1-5498-9525-784902e9bcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-307029" :question/problem "![](images/85/85648d02ef153e0d2b0019d66f647c6160caafbc25b16d5ccc370a962ec38e5a.png)\n\nA call center operated for twenty-four hours, and its time and handling rate are shown on the graph above. According to the graph, which of the following statements is **not** true concerning the call center’s operation?\n\n1. The handling rate increased at a constant rate during the first four hours.\n2. The handling rate was constant for eight consecutive hours.\n3. The handling rate reached its maximum during the last eight hours.\n4. The handling rate decreased at a constant rate during the last four hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b9c0ab8-60db-5c9c-a481-2fd656b8e224" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Margin of Error of a Population Proportion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050899 :topic/math-academy-id 6587 :topic/title "Interpreting Margin of Error"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85cd3725-e340-540e-bdb2-ec975cb540cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227354} {:answer/id #uuid "92d4b6a3-a0f7-5734-b61b-9d4975c7ae5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227355} {:answer/id #uuid "95ccfe55-cb9d-5a8c-87e3-e381ceb98885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227356} {:answer/id #uuid "15f6a550-9f76-5150-8144-a3610360dfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227357} {:answer/id #uuid "792d50c3-8abc-56ff-9e97-5e764dc14318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227358}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a4bfa-24c2-5257-8cdd-065aa0b753c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227353}] :question/id #uuid "0056fc25-1c70-559a-a0ee-f7448089010e" :question/math-academy-id "q-308138" :question/problem "A city transit agency wants to estimate the percentage of buses that completed their routes on schedule on a particular day. A random sample of $560$ buses from that day was inspected. Based on the sample, it is estimated that $72.5%$ of all buses that day were on schedule, with a margin of error of $3.1%$.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions about all buses that day is most reasonable?\n\n1. It is plausible that between $69.4%$ and $75.6%$ of the buses were on schedule.\n2. Exactly $72.5%$ of the buses were on schedule.\n3. It is plausible that more than $75.6%$ of the buses were on schedule.\n4. Exactly $3.1%$ of the buses were on schedule."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a18dab-217c-537c-95d5-173e5361e892" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Statistics Are Unaffected by a Data Transformation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049582 :topic/math-academy-id 6602 :topic/title "Comparing Statistical Effects of Data Change"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98604ca1-47b9-5cee-a4db-eb64e757e14e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227692} {:answer/id #uuid "e4eaea0a-4fe