nt:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98285d76-61c2-5a8d-b241-e3e29c333118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228074} {:answer/id #uuid "03adc7e2-530f-5d76-90a4-30a90cd17832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228075} {:answer/id #uuid "51a80c7c-b04c-505d-a699-bf339bdb8bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228076} {:answer/id #uuid "564ea6a7-9599-517c-9298-8205a9814679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228077} {:answer/id #uuid "6f576e7b-ef0f-55d7-b095-edbcfcc5d273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228078}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1baf0ac-4bf9-56bf-9a40-1e6b18e17dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228073}] :question/id #uuid "3181b002-28a8-5b0e-b554-488c8ba4e4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-332571" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 3$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(3Δx) = \\frac{1 - 3Δx/2}{1 + 3Δx/2}$.\n2. The stability region is $\\mid \\frac{9}{2}(Δx - \\frac{2}{3})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 2.5$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c495fcbe-46e4-5570-a5d1-28127f914a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228101} {:answer/id #uuid "db63787a-f64d-520f-b951-801e1f9c89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228102} {:answer/id #uuid "f9029063-2276-5c5e-aaae-5419b3c1391e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228103} {:answer/id #uuid "62130c11-c8cc-5bfd-aa1b-21009a57c496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} {:answer/id #uuid "75075a4d-6bc9-5c33-a9f0-607303832709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228105}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0b55e392-d0cf-5935-be82-e8780c5e7cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228100}] :question/id #uuid "e90b20b3-e8ec-5eff-9813-0425b7acc64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332744" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.4$\n2. $λΔx = 1$\n3. $λΔx = 2$\n4. $λΔx = 3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n$R(λΔ) = 1 - λΔx + ((λΔx)^{2})/(2) - ((λΔx)^{3})/(6) + ((λΔx)^{4})/(24)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb954d41-7aec-50ff-9088-73366520bd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228113} {:answer/id #uuid "e878630a-75fc-5919-9018-2b107367a072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228114} {:answer/id #uuid "20a73ed5-b452-545c-a27d-31335b998f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228115} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3e450d-45fa-58ed-b194-fc735133412c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228116} {:answer/id #uuid "650f038e-f1e1-5ea6-9620-dcf6fe0029da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650f038e-f1e1-5ea6-9620-dcf6fe0029da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228117} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0de205-3871-5589-99ce-a25b0449ebb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d6b24a2-a25a-5fce-957a-e0f07d99344e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333079" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050185 :topic/math-academy-id 6692 :topic/title "d'Alembert Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98c79649-7c43-576d-ad8a-5fcbceb3351d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228155} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ed5ad2-58c5-5600-9cbf-ec86eae16a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228156} {:answer/id #uuid "0f70f02a-f8c5-5c74-807e-eb4cfccb6729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228157} {:answer/id #uuid "197eb2df-f819-5a4a-9672-d07ba2ee5200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218622815